            <ArticleTitle>New insights into lung diseases using hyperpolarized gas MRI.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>303-13</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.rx.2014.12.011</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0033-8338(15)00012-0</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Hyperpolarized (HP) gases are a new class of contrast agents that permit to obtain high temporal and spatial resolution magnetic resonance images (MRI) of the lung airspaces. HP gas MRI has become important research tool not only for morphological and functional evaluation of normal pulmonary physiology but also for regional quantification of pathologic changes occurring in several lung diseases. The purpose of this work is to provide an introduction to MRI using HP noble gases, describing both the basic principles of the technique and the new information about lung disease provided by clinical studies with this method. The applications of the technique in normal subjects, smoking related lung disease, asthma, and cystic fibrosis are reviewed.</AbstractText>
